[[316,125,"Inadequate application of police restraint training","positional asphyxia risk",""],[317,125,"Failure to apply the National Decision Model","reassessment failure",""],[318,125,"Ineffective welfare monitoring","unmonitored breathing deterioration",""],[319,125,"Failure of officers to \"speak up and speak out\"","silence on improper restraint",""],[320,125,"Inadequate communication and handover","information gap on restraint duration",""],[321,125,"Inappropriate prioritisation of transport over welfare","welfare ignored during FLACS application",""],[323,125,"Inadequate Assessment","assessment inability due to unresponsiveness",""],[324,125,"Failure to Escalate Concerns","unvoiced clinical concern",""],[325,125,"Unsafe Communication Practices","reliance on verbal communication",""],[326,125,"Inappropriate Discharge Recommendation","risky discharge suggestion",""],[327,125,"Non-existent Documentation Referenced in Policy","policy references missing form",""],[328,125,"Misleading Discharge Documentation","document misinterpreted as official discharge",""],[330,125,"Inadequate Discharge Review Process","delayed final review decision",""],[862,335,"Lack of training and guidance for frontline officers on conducting searches of premises in missing person cases. Evidence given by the Police at the Inquest indicated that there is no structured training or clear operational guidance on what constitutes an adequate search, including checking all areas of a property where a person could reasonably be found.","inadequate search of premises in missing person cases",""],[863,335,"Absence of protocols for escalating safety concerns during searches. The officer who undertook the search felt unsafe due to the cluttered environment but did not escalate this concern or request additional resources to enable an adequate search to be carried out. There appears to be no guidance on when and how officers should escalate such issues.","escalation of safety concerns during searches",""],[864,335,"risk that future deaths could occur unless action is taken","future deaths",""],[4382,1647,"Whether sufficient steps have been taken to ensure that frontline staff have a clear understanding of domestic abuse and coercive and controlling behaviour - to think not just about physical abuse, but also about controlling behaviour and how that may escalate","lack of clear understanding of domestic abuse and coercive controlling behaviour among frontline staff",""],[4383,1647,"I am concerned that Thames Valley Police's DASH risk assessment form (called a DOM5) does not make it sufficiently clear that the definition of \"serious harm\" can include psychological harm from coercive and controlling behaviour","ambiguous definition of serious harm in DASH risk assessment form",""],[4894,1840,"material; and d) offer KS adequate mental healthcare in the community and secondary mental healthcare in prison","inadequate mental healthcare provision",""],[4895,1840,"The failure to maintain and disseminate an adequate intelligence picture","intelligence dissemination failure",""],[4896,1840,"a failure by CTPSE to share relevant information with MI5 - see","information siloing with MI5",""],[4897,1840,"The failure to handle the intelligence concerning KS appropriately had a notable impact on the Prevent, Pathfinder and MAPPA processes","process failure in risk management schemes",""],[9792,3735,"There was extensive BWV footage in this case, which both I and the jury were able to see multiple times. It appeared to me, (and presumably to the jury, given their conclusion), that the officers at the scene were trying to take Neal's welfare into account. The real issue was around their training. Whilst there may not have been any realistic or practicable alternative to restraining Neal, at least initially, it was clear from the evidence that none of the officers recalled their training which","prolonged restraint",""],[9793,3735,"The training gives no guidance as to what constitutes","undefined prolonged restraint",""],[9794,3735,"One of the witnesses questioned whether the guidelines applied at all if somebody is under arrest, particularly regarding","guideline applicability uncertainty",""],[9795,3735,"The College of Policing slides regarding ABD state that a","incorrect category 1 expectation",""],[9796,3735,"The guidelines also refer to providing","misunderstanding of chemical sedation",""],[9797,3735,"Is there a better way for the College of Policing to ensure that the training has worked and is embedded?","training effectiveness verification",""],[12729,4928,"One of the reasons that the drugs SOP was introduced by Thames Valley Police on 1* July 2016 was to prevent exactly what happened in this case. That is (one of) the reasons that the SOP requires an officer to observe from within the cell.","officer not observing from within the cell",""],[13223,5126,"The concern that | have is mirrored in Chief Superintendent Bunt's statement paragraph 57 where it states that the current partnership agreement does not adequately cover incidents such as this one. The problem is the not uncommon situation where a person is experiencing a mental health crisis and there is an imminent need for agencies to respond. If the person is experiencing the mental health crisis is in their home or another private place, police powers are limited. | recognise there is a","gap in partnership agreement for mental health crisis response",""]]